 "why": "It did the opposite: with all-star's finishing and puck movement pro's opponents scored LESS and every hand won more. Goals against a minute: planning hand 0.69 to 0.42 / 0.62 to 0.44 / 0.57 to 0.53 (novice / regular / good), thirdjam's hands 0.77 to 0.50 / 0.69 to 0.48 / 0.65 to 0.54. Wins: thirdjam's novice 16.7 to 40 % (+23.3 +-8.0), regular 60 to 75 (+15 +-8.2); planning hand 58.3 to 68.3 / 88.3 to 91.7 / 93.3 to 98.3. A bot that winds its shot up, dekes or looks for a pass holds the puck for the moments in which it is stripped; at all-star the hunt and the quicker stick pay for that, at pro nothing does. The regular planning hand moved away from the band (88 to 92 %), so it is not kept.",
 "notes": "What it taught: \"better play\" is not a list of all-star's switches. For a bot the tap from where it stands IS the better play at pro's pace of pressure. The band has to come from how pro presses (the owner's own ladder is an aggression ladder, R-0041): E12. Epoch k8-4."
